  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "herbalife protein powder",
      "personalized protein powder herbalife",
      "herbalife shake and protein powder",
      "herbalife formula 3",
      "herbalife formula 1",
      "herbalife protein drink mix",
      "is herbalife third party tested"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Meal Replacement Picks",
    link: "/best-meal-replacement-shake/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Herbalife Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      For anyone handed a Herbalife shake plan by a distributor, or eyeing those tubs on Amazon, this settles what is in Formula 1 and the Personalized Protein Powder, what each gram of protein costs, and whether the core products are independently tested.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#which-product">Formula 1 vs Formula 3</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in It</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#amazon">Amazon and Distributors</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs">Herbalife vs Alternatives</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Herbalife protein powder is a low-dose soy and whey add-in: the Personalized Protein Powder gives 5 g of protein per 6 g tablespoon, and the Formula 1 shake it is meant for gives 9 g per serving with water, per Herbalife's US labels. The answer changes if you buy Herbalife24, the only line with NSF Certified for Sport products. Below: labels, sweeteners, testing, cost per 25 g, the distributor model and Amazon resellers.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Herbalife Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Herbalife's protein powder is a small-dose booster, not a stand-alone protein shake. The Personalized Protein Powder (called Formula 3 in many countries) gives 5 g of soy and whey protein per tablespoon, and the Formula 1 shake it is designed for gives 9 g of soy protein per serving with water, per Herbalife's US labels at the time of writing. Stack them the way Herbalife suggests and you reach 17 to 24 g of protein, at well over $5 per 25 g at retail.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you already work with a distributor you trust, you like the Formula 1 flavors, and the added vitamins and coaching matter more to you than price. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you want the most protein for your money, want an independently certified core product, or would be buying from an Amazon reseller with no idea where the tub has been.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Herbalife product (US)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sweetener</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g*</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Formula 1 shake, French Vanilla</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">9 g (water) / 17 g (nonfat milk)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90 / 170</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fructose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $5.72</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Personalized Protein Powder</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g per tablespoon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $4.46</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein Drink Mix, Vanilla</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $5.72</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Herbalife24 Enhanced Protein Powder</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $3.83</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            *Cost per 25 g uses the retail prices on Herbalife's US site at the time of writing and the protein figures on its US labels. Distributors and members pay less, so the price you are quoted may be lower. Only the Herbalife24 product in this table carries NSF Certified for Sport, per Herbalife and the NSF database.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Herbalife's Two Core Powders and the Closest Amazon Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first two cards are Herbalife's own products, priced at the retail price on Herbalife's US site at the time of writing. They have no Amazon link because Herbalife does not authorize Amazon sales. The other three are Amazon products that do the same jobs for less money per gram of protein.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="which-product" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is Herbalife Formula 3 the Same as the Personalized Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Outside the US, yes: in the UK, Europe, Asia and much of Africa, Herbalife sells its unflavored whey and soy booster as Formula 3 Personalised Protein Powder. In the US, the same type of product is sold simply as Personalized Protein Powder, and the name Formula 3 belongs to Cell Activator, a capsule supplement. Herbalife's own US Formula 1 page recommends pairing the shake with Formula 2 Multivitamin Complex and Formula 3 Cell Activator, which shows how the US numbering works.
          </p>

          <p>
            That naming split causes real confusion. People searching for "herbalife formula 3" from the US often expect a protein powder and end up on a pill, and Amazon resellers mix both naming systems in their titles. If the tub says Formula 3 and Personalised (British spelling) on it, you are almost certainly looking at an import made for another market, with a label written to that country's rules.
          </p>

          <p>
            The formulas also differ by country. Herbalife's Namibia page lists its Formula 3 as about 75% soy protein isolate and 24.5% whey protein concentrate, while the US label lists soy protein isolate, whey protein concentrate, natural flavor and silicon dioxide without percentages. Sizes differ too: 240 g and 200 g tubs are common abroad, while the US canister is 12.7 oz (360 g) with 60 servings. The protein per tablespoon is about the same everywhere, roughly 5 g in 6 g of powder.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where do Formula 1, Protein Drink Mix and Herbalife24 fit?</h3>

          <p>
            Herbalife sells four products people call its protein powder. Formula 1 is the meal shake. Personalized Protein Powder is the unflavored booster you stir into Formula 1. Protein Drink Mix is a flavored soy booster with 15 g of protein per 28 g serving, which Herbalife says lifts a Formula 1 shake to 24 g of protein when mixed with water. Herbalife24 is the sports line, and its Enhanced Protein Powder is the only one of the four built like a conventional protein powder, with 24 g of protein per 32 g serving from five milk proteins.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the phrase "herbalife shake and protein powder" usually means Formula 1 plus one of the two boosters. Herbalife's own instructions pair them, and distributors often sell them as a bundle. The rest of this review treats that pair as the main product and brings in Herbalife24 where it changes the answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Herbalife Formula 1 and the Personalized Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Formula 1 French Vanilla is mostly soy protein isolate and fructose, followed by fiber, flavor, minerals and a long vitamin premix, per the ingredient list on Herbalife's US site at the time of writing. A 25 g serving gives 90 calories, 1 g of fat, 13 g of carbohydrate, 3 g of fiber, 9 g of total sugar (8 g of it added), 95 mg of sodium and 9 g of protein. It lists 21 vitamins and minerals, most at 20 to 60% of the daily value per serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein comes mainly from soy, with some calcium caseinate and casein further down the list, so the shake contains both milk and soy. The thickeners and fibers include cellulose, corn bran, guar gum, carrageenan, inulin, psyllium and citrus pectin, and there is a small enzyme blend (protease, bromelain and papain). Herbalife says Formula 1 is gluten-free except for the Cookies 'n Cream and Mint Chocolate flavors.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Personalized Protein Powder has the shortest label Herbalife sells: soy protein isolate, whey protein concentrate, natural flavor and silicon dioxide (an anti-caking agent). One 6 g tablespoon gives 20 calories, 0 g of fat, 0 g of carbohydrate, 50 mg of sodium and 5 g of protein, per the US label. It is unsweetened, which is why Herbalife suggests stirring it into soups, yogurt and sauces as well as shakes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which sweeteners does Herbalife use?</h3>

          <p>
            Formula 1 French Vanilla is sweetened mainly with fructose plus a little honey powder, per the US ingredient list, which is why it carries 8 g of added sugar per serving. The Protein Drink Mix uses sucralose, the same artificial sweetener found in many sports powders, and has 1 g of added sugar. The Personalized Protein Powder has no sweetener at all, and Herbalife24 Enhanced Protein Powder uses stevia leaf extract. Sweeteners vary by flavor, so check the panel on the exact canister you buy.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE LABEL MATH</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">9 g of protein, 9 g of sugar</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Made with water, a serving of Formula 1 French Vanilla has as many grams of sugar as grams of protein, per the US label. Mixing it with nonfat milk lifts protein to 17 g but also lifts total sugar to 22 g, since milk adds its own lactose.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Herbalife's own page points out that 25 g of soy protein a day, as part of a diet low in saturated fat and cholesterol, may reduce the risk of heart disease, and that one serving of Formula 1 provides 9 g. It also cites one clinical study on US Formula 1, and others on similar products, showing that two shakes a day within a reduced-calorie diet plus moderate exercise helped people lose weight. That is a result for a calorie-controlled plan, not evidence that the shake is better than any other meal replacement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Herbalife Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some Herbalife products are; the core shake and protein boosters are not certified. Herbalife says Herbalife24 Formula 1 Sport, Herbalife24 Rebuild Strength and Herbalife24 Enhanced Protein Powder are NSF Certified for Sport, and the NSF Certified for Sport database lists US versions of all three, along with several other Herbalife24 products such as CR7 Drive, BCAAs and Achieve protein bars.
          </p>

          <p>
            Herbalife spells out what that certification means on its product pages: tested to be clean of athletic-banned substances, verified to meet label claims and contaminant test criteria, and made in a facility audited for good manufacturing practice. That matches NSF's own description of the program. It is the strongest testing claim in Herbalife's range, and it is product-specific: the badge covers the listed Herbalife24 products, not the brand as a whole.
          </p>

          <p>
            We did not find the regular Formula 1 shake, the Personalized Protein Powder or the Protein Drink Mix in the NSF Certified for Sport database, and their US product pages do not mention NSF, Informed Sport, Informed Choice or any other independent certification at the time of writing. Herbalife describes its own in-house quality testing, but that is the company testing itself. If independent verification matters to you, that distinction is the whole point.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does Herbalife publish heavy metal results?</h3>

          <p>
            Not that we could find for its core shakes. Herbalife does not post batch results for lead, cadmium or arsenic on its US product pages, and the core products carry no third-party seal that would cover contaminants. NSF's contaminant screening applies to the certified Herbalife24 products only. If metals are your main worry, our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> covers brands that publish their numbers, and <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF-certified protein powders</a> lists the badge holders.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more caveat applies to tubs bought outside Herbalife's own channels. Any testing Herbalife does only covers product that stayed in its supply chain. Herbalife has publicly warned about counterfeit and tampered product sold through unauthorized online sellers, so a certification or quality claim tells you nothing about a canister of unknown origin.
          </p>
        </div>

