        <div id="amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Can You Buy Herbalife on Amazon, and Is It Safe?
          </h2>

          <p>
            You can find Herbalife on Amazon, but Herbalife does not authorize those sales. Its US FAQ says independent distributors may not offer or facilitate sales through any auction or e-commerce site, naming eBay and Amazon, and may only sell online through platforms Herbalife creates, such as the free branded web stores it gives distributors. Every Herbalife listing on Amazon is therefore a third-party seller operating outside Herbalife's rules.
          </p>

          <p>
            When we searched, Amazon had listings for Formula 1 French Vanilla at $65.96, Protein Drink Mix from $78.63 and a 200 g Personalized Protein Powder at $43.95. The French Vanilla price was above Herbalife's own $61.75 retail price, and 200 g is not the US canister size, which suggests an import. Bundles of Formula 1 with the protein powder ran $115 to $175.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What are the risks of buying from a reseller?</h3>

          <p>
            The main risks are age, storage and authenticity. A reseller's tub may be close to its best-by date, may have sat in a hot warehouse, or may be a version made for another country with different ingredients and a label in another language. Herbalife has issued public warnings about counterfeit and tampered product sold online, and says it pursues enforcement against unauthorized sellers. You also lose Herbalife's customer satisfaction and refund policy, which applies to product bought through its own channels.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want Herbalife, buy it from Herbalife's site, a distributor's Herbalife-hosted store or a distributor in person. If the point was simply a convenient meal or protein shake you can order on Amazon, a brand that sells there directly, such as Orgain or Optimum Nutrition, removes the guesswork.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How does the distributor model change the price?</h3>

          <p>
            Herbalife is a multi-level marketing company: products move through a network of independent distributors who earn retail margins and payments tied to the volume of the people they recruit. That structure is part of why the retail price is high. In 2016, Herbalife agreed to pay $200 million and restructure its US business in a settlement with the Federal Trade Commission, which had alleged that its compensation rewarded recruiting over retail sales. Buying a tub is not joining the business, but expect some distributors to pitch both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Do Buyers Say About Herbalife Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers mostly like the taste of Formula 1, and flavor is the most common reason people say they keep buying it. In retail reviews and distributor feedback, Cookies 'n Cream, French Vanilla and Dutch Chocolate come up most often as favorites, and buyers often describe the shake as sweet and dessert-like, which fits a label led by soy protein and fructose. We have not tasted the products; this is what buyers report.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixing depends heavily on the liquid. Buyers who follow Herbalife's directions, 2 scoops in 8 fl oz of nonfat milk or soy milk, usually report a smooth, milkshake-like texture. Buyers who mix it with water often describe it as thin or slightly chalky, and some say it settles if left to stand. Herbalife itself recommends shaking the canister before use because the contents settle, and many distributors blend it with ice and fruit.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Personalized Protein Powder gets fewer flavor comments because it is meant to disappear. Buyers report that one or two tablespoons stir into a Formula 1 shake without changing the taste much, and that larger amounts can make the shake thicker and slightly grainy. Some buyers notice a faint soy note when they use it in plain water or yogurt.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What do buyers complain about?</h3>

          <p>
            Price is the most common complaint, followed by the protein dose: buyers who compare labels are often surprised that a serving made with water has only 9 g. Other repeated complaints are the sugar content, the soy base for people avoiding soy, and pressure from distributors to buy bundles, add tea and aloe, or sign up for an ongoing order. Buyers who bought on Amazon add complaints about short dates and packaging that looked different from what they were used to.
          </p>
        </div>

