        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Herbalife Protein Powder, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Herbalife makes sense for a narrow group: people who already have a distributor they trust, enjoy the Formula 1 flavors, and value a structured plan with coaching more than the cost per gram. For that buyer, Formula 1 mixed with nonfat milk plus a tablespoon or two of the Personalized Protein Powder gives a sweet shake with 22 to 27 g of protein and a full vitamin premix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Athletes who are drug-tested should only use the Herbalife24 products listed in the NSF Certified for Sport database, and should check the specific product and flavor there before buying. The core Formula 1 shake and the protein boosters carry no such certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip Herbalife if you are trying to keep protein cheap, want a high protein dose in one scoop, avoid soy, or want to limit added sugar. A plain soy isolate gives the Personalized Protein Powder's protein for about one-fifth of the price, and most whey powders give 24 g per scoop for under $1.50 per 25 g. Our <a href="/best-soy-protein-powder/">soy protein powder guide</a> lists options if you like the soy base.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some people should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before using any meal-replacement plan, Herbalife included: people with kidney disease, anyone pregnant or breastfeeding, people with diabetes adjusting medication, and anyone with a soy or milk allergy (Formula 1 and the protein powders contain both). If you are unsure how much protein you need at all, our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein calculator guide</a> is a better starting point than any shake plan.
          </p>
        </div>

